1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,375
Welcome to Session five.

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:07,725
Session four left two problems unsolved, and this session is about both of them.

3
00:00:07,725 --> 00:00:09,800
The first is scale.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:17,925
Needleman-Wunsch and Smith-Waterman find the provably optimal alignment between two sequences, and that guarantee is genuine.

5
00:00:17,925 --> 00:00:27,150
But the cost grows with the product of the two lengths, and a database search is not two sequences — it is one query against well over a hundred million.

6
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:32,100
The guarantee becomes unaffordable, and we have to decide what to give up.

7
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,750
The second is sensitivity.

8
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:38,150
Session four aligned two sequences at a time.

9
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:45,875
But we saw that a protein family carries information no pair does: which positions evolution keeps, and which it lets vary.

10
00:00:45,875 --> 00:00:53,625
If we have a whole family, we ought to be able to use it to recognise a relative too distant for any pairwise comparison to catch.

11
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:56,175
So the session has three blocks.

12
00:00:56,175 --> 00:01:02,000
The first is BLAST — the heuristic that makes database search possible, and what it costs you.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,150
The second builds a profile from a family, as a position-specific scoring matrix, and iterates the search with it.

14
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:20,300
The third goes one step further, to profile hidden Markov models, which model not only which residues occur at each position but where the family tolerates insertions and deletions.

15
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:31,350
The thread running through all three is a single idea: the more the search knows about the family, the more distant the relatives it can find — and the more carefully that family has to be built.

16
00:01:31,950 --> 00:01:37,575
Here is the picture to hold in mind for the whole session, and we will come back to it at the end.

17
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:40,225
On the left, ordinary BLAST.

18
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:51,100
One query sequence, and every position of it scored with the same substitution matrix — the same BLOSUM sixty-two numbers at position three as at position ninety.

19
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,475
The matrix knows about proteins in general.

20
00:01:54,475 --> 00:01:57,275
It knows nothing about this family.

21
00:01:57,275 --> 00:01:59,425
On the right, a profile.

22
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:05,600
Every position has its own scores, derived from what the family actually does at that position.

23
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:12,950
Where the family is rigidly conserved, the profile demands the conserved residue and penalises everything else heavily.

24
00:02:12,950 --> 00:02:16,775
Where the family varies freely, the profile is tolerant.

25
00:02:16,775 --> 00:02:23,350
The same sequence, described by a model of its relatives rather than by a general-purpose average.

26
00:02:23,350 --> 00:02:28,625
That difference is what lets a profile reach relatives a single sequence cannot.

27
00:02:28,625 --> 00:02:38,825
And it is also where the risk enters, because a profile is built from sequences you chose, and if you choose badly the profile describes something other than the family you meant.

28
00:02:39,425 --> 00:02:41,025
First block.

29
00:02:41,025 --> 00:02:46,000
How do you search a hundred million sequences, and what do you give up to do it?

30
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:51,175
Start with the size of the problem, because the numbers settle the argument.

31
00:02:51,175 --> 00:03:01,200
Session four's dynamic programming costs on the order of the product of the two sequence lengths — for two proteins a few hundred residues long, around ten thousand cells.

32
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,250
On any modern machine that is instant.

33
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:07,875
There is nothing wrong with dynamic programming for a pair.

34
00:03:07,875 --> 00:03:11,625
Now put a hundred million sequences on the other side.

35
00:03:11,625 --> 00:03:16,600
You are no longer filling one matrix, you are filling a hundred million of them.

36
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:27,475
The arithmetic is no longer instant, and the important part is that the problem keeps getting worse: every database release makes it worse, while the query stays the same size.

37
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:32,950
So a database search needs something faster, and faster means giving something up.

38
00:03:32,950 --> 00:03:40,025
BLAST — the Basic Local Alignment Search Tool — gives up the guarantee of optimality, and buys speed with it.

39
00:03:40,025 --> 00:03:42,725
That trade is the whole design.

40
00:03:42,725 --> 00:03:51,925
The insight is that most of the database is irrelevant to any given query, and you can find out which parts are irrelevant far more cheaply than by aligning them.

41
00:03:51,925 --> 00:04:01,850
So BLAST first looks for short near-exact matches, which are cheap to find, and spends the expensive alignment step only on the regions those matches point at.

42
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:07,750
Keep the shape of that bargain in mind, because we will see it cost us an answer later in this block.

43
00:04:08,350 --> 00:04:13,825
BLAST runs in three stages, and each one passes on far less than it received.

44
00:04:13,825 --> 00:04:22,400
Seeding: break the query into short words, work out every word that would score well against each of them, and scan the database for those words.

45
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:26,150
Cheap per position, and it discards almost everything.

46
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:32,850
Extension: take each seed that survived and grow it outwards in both directions into a full local alignment.

47
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:37,150
More expensive, but now running on a tiny fraction of the database.

48
00:04:37,150 --> 00:04:42,800
Evaluation: ask whether the resulting alignment scores better than chance would produce.

49
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:46,375
This is where a score becomes a claim about biology.

50
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:48,825
The funnel shape is the point.

51
00:04:48,825 --> 00:04:55,300
Each stage is more expensive per candidate than the last, and each receives far fewer candidates.

52
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:58,325
That is what makes the whole thing affordable.

53
00:04:58,325 --> 00:05:18,575
Real production BLAST adds refinements on top: the two-hit rule, where a seed is extended only when a second seed lies close by on the same diagonal; masking of low-complexity regions so that runs of a single residue do not generate spurious seeds; gapped extension; and corrections for unusual amino-acid composition.

54
00:05:18,575 --> 00:05:22,950
We will meet the two-hit rule again shortly, when it costs us something.

55
00:05:22,950 --> 00:05:26,300
But none of these refinements changes the logic.

56
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,875
Seed, extend, evaluate.

57
00:05:29,475 --> 00:05:32,950
The first stage turns the query into short words.

58
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:41,750
For proteins a word is usually three residues, so a query of length two hundred contains a hundred and ninety-eight overlapping three-letter words.

59
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:48,225
Now, BLAST does not search for exact occurrences of those words only, and the reason is biological.

60
00:05:48,225 --> 00:05:57,850
A genuinely related sequence may well have substituted a residue inside any given three-residue window without the relationship being any less real.

61
00:05:57,850 --> 00:06:03,425
Searching for exact matches only would miss precisely the distant relatives we most want.

62
00:06:03,425 --> 00:06:16,475
So instead, for each query word, BLAST uses a substitution matrix — BLOSUM sixty-two, from Session four — to precompute every three-letter combination scoring above a threshold T against it.

63
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:20,600
Those are the neighbour words, and all of them are treated as seeds.

64
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:24,400
Take the query word L-W-G as the example.

65
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:27,375
Against itself it scores twenty-one.

66
00:06:27,375 --> 00:06:40,350
I-W-G scores nineteen, and M-W-G also scores nineteen, because leucine-to-isoleucine and leucine-to-methionine are conservative substitutions that BLOSUM sixty-two scores positively.

67
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:49,200
V-W-G gets eighteen, F-W-G seventeen, and then a group at sixteen — A-W-G, C-W-G, T-W-G, Y-W-G.

68
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:55,050
With a typical threshold of T equals twelve, forty-nine words clear the bar in total.

69
00:06:55,050 --> 00:06:57,650
Notice the pattern in that list.

70
00:06:57,650 --> 00:07:04,000
The tryptophan and the glycine are untouched in every one of them; it is only the leucine that varies.

71
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:11,700
That is BLOSUM sixty-two talking: leucine has many acceptable substitutes, tryptophan has almost none.

72
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:15,950
The neighbourhood of a word is shaped by the chemistry of its residues.

73
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:18,000
And T is a dial.

74
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,675
Lower it and you get more neighbour words, more seeds, more sensitivity and a slower search.

75
00:07:23,675 --> 00:07:26,300
Raise it and you get the opposite.

76
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:30,475
Sensitivity against speed, exposed as a parameter.

77
00:07:31,075 --> 00:07:37,475
Now the trick that actually makes BLAST fast, and it is worth being precise about where the speed comes from.

78
00:07:37,475 --> 00:07:44,375
All those query words and their neighbours go into a lookup table — a hash table, with the word itself as the key.

79
00:07:44,375 --> 00:07:52,525
The defining property of a hash table is that looking something up costs the same whether the table holds a thousand entries or a million.

80
00:07:52,525 --> 00:07:55,325
The table is built once per query.

81
00:07:55,325 --> 00:08:07,325
Then BLAST reads each database sequence once, start to end, and at every position asks a single question: is this three-letter word in my table, and if so, which query positions does it seed?

82
00:08:07,325 --> 00:08:10,250
That question is one table lookup.

83
00:08:10,250 --> 00:08:18,500
Not an alignment, not a comparison against the query, not anything whose cost grows with the query's length — one constant-time lookup.

84
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:24,250
So scanning the database is linear in the size of the database, and the constant is small.

85
00:08:24,250 --> 00:08:28,225
This is the answer to "where does BLAST's speed come from".

86
00:08:28,225 --> 00:08:32,950
Not from a cleverer alignment algorithm; the alignment is ordinary.

87
00:08:32,950 --> 00:08:44,600
It comes from replacing the question "how well does the query align here?" with the question "is this word in a table?" — and only asking the expensive question at the few places where the cheap one says yes.

88
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:48,250
A seed is a three-letter coincidence.

89
00:08:48,250 --> 00:08:56,525
With twenty amino acids, three-letter coincidences happen constantly by chance, so a seed is not yet evidence of anything.

90
00:08:56,525 --> 00:08:59,350
Stage two grows it to find out.

91
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:10,450
BLAST extends the seed outwards in both directions, keeping a running score as it goes, using the same substitution-matrix logic as Session four but without filling a matrix.

92
00:09:10,450 --> 00:09:14,700
The question is when to stop, and the answer is the X-drop rule.

93
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:20,150
BLAST tracks two numbers: the running score right now, and the best score seen so far.

94
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:30,825
If the running score ever falls more than X below that best score, extension stops — on the reasoning that an alignment which has given up that much ground is very unlikely to recover.

95
00:09:30,825 --> 00:09:41,325
The worked example uses two short English strings, scoring plus one for a matching character and minus one for a mismatch, ignoring spaces, with X equal to five.

96
00:09:41,325 --> 00:09:47,225
The query reads "The quick brown fox jumps" and the database "The quiet brown cat purrs".

97
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:49,425
Follow the running score.

98
00:09:49,425 --> 00:09:51,500
It climbs through "The qu".

99
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:58,200
Then "ick" against "iet" mismatches and it dips — but the dip is smaller than X, so extension survives it.

100
00:09:58,200 --> 00:10:03,925
That matters: a real alignment can contain a bad patch, and the rule is tolerant of one.

101
00:10:03,925 --> 00:10:09,350
Then "brown" against "brown" matches all the way and the score climbs to a peak of nine.

102
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:14,775
After that, "fox jump" against "cat purr" diverges and the score falls steadily.

103
00:10:14,775 --> 00:10:19,325
Once it has fallen five below the peak of nine, extension stops.

104
00:10:19,325 --> 00:10:21,975
Now the detail people get wrong.

105
00:10:21,975 --> 00:10:26,275
The reported alignment is not what was scored when extension stopped.

106
00:10:26,275 --> 00:10:29,875
It is trimmed back to where the best score actually occurred.

107
00:10:29,875 --> 00:10:36,225
So the output pairs "The quick brown" with "The quiet brown" and leaves out the diverging tail entirely.

108
00:10:36,225 --> 00:10:41,625
The drop-off rule decides when to stop looking; the peak decides what to report.

109
00:10:41,625 --> 00:10:43,575
Two different things.

110
00:10:43,575 --> 00:10:51,900
An extended seed scoring above a cutoff is called a high-scoring segment pair, an HSP, and that is the unit BLAST reports.

111
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:59,550
One pair of sequences can give several — two proteins sharing two domains will often give one HSP per domain.

112
00:11:00,150 --> 00:11:07,775
This slide is the statistical result underneath every E-value you will ever read, and it is worth a minute of your attention.

113
00:11:07,775 --> 00:11:14,450
Here are four thousand alignments of shuffled sequences — no relationship at all — with their scores plotted.

114
00:11:14,450 --> 00:11:16,500
Look at the left panel.

115
00:11:16,500 --> 00:11:19,275
The distribution is not symmetric.

116
00:11:19,275 --> 00:11:23,050
It is skewed, with a distinct tail running out to the right.

117
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:26,425
That is not an artefact; it is necessary.

118
00:11:26,425 --> 00:11:29,850
A local alignment score is not one measurement.

119
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:35,200
It is the best of an enormous number of possible sub-alignments between the two sequences.

120
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:42,500
And the maximum of many random tries does not follow a bell curve, however nicely behaved the individual tries are.

121
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:48,575
It follows an extreme-value distribution — a Gumbel — which is skewed, with a long right tail.

122
00:11:48,575 --> 00:11:52,800
Now the right panel, which is why this is not a technicality.

123
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:59,225
Same data, and the question: what are the chances of a chance alignment beating a score of twenty-five?

124
00:11:59,225 --> 00:12:02,325
The measurements say about one in a thousand.

125
00:12:02,325 --> 00:12:07,925
A normal distribution fitted with the same mean and spread says about one in a million.

126
00:12:07,925 --> 00:12:13,450
That is wrong by three orders of magnitude, and it is wrong in the dangerous direction.

127
00:12:13,450 --> 00:12:21,325
The normal curve would tell you a hit that chance produces once in a thousand tries is overwhelming, once-in-a-million evidence.

128
00:12:21,325 --> 00:12:31,450
If you search a database of a million sequences, the difference between those two numbers is the difference between expecting a thousand chance hits and expecting one.

129
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:40,025
Karlin and Altschul proved the correct result in nineteen ninety, and the constants K and lambda in the E-value formula come directly from it.

130
00:12:40,025 --> 00:12:44,800
Every E-value in every BLAST output you will ever read rests on this.

131
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:49,425
From that distribution follows the number we actually use.

132
00:12:49,425 --> 00:12:55,875
The E-value is the number of alignments scoring at least S that you would expect to see purely by chance.

133
00:12:55,875 --> 00:13:01,525
The formula is E equals K times m times n times e to the minus lambda S.

134
00:13:01,525 --> 00:13:03,375
Take it apart.

135
00:13:03,375 --> 00:13:14,875
S is the score, and it enters through a negative exponential, so a better score drives E down exponentially — which is why real E-values are written as ten to the minus thirty-something.

136
00:13:14,875 --> 00:13:18,875
m and n are the lengths of the query and of the database.

137
00:13:18,875 --> 00:13:30,175
K and lambda are constants belonging to the scoring system, the substitution matrix and gap penalties together, and real BLAST output reports them as the Karlin-Altschul statistics.

138
00:13:30,175 --> 00:13:33,250
Two consequences matter in practice.

139
00:13:33,250 --> 00:13:35,450
First, read the units.

140
00:13:35,450 --> 00:13:39,450
An E-value is an expected count, not a probability.

141
00:13:39,450 --> 00:13:46,275
An E-value of five means you would expect about five hits this good by chance, so finding one proves nothing.

142
00:13:46,275 --> 00:13:51,600
An E-value of ten to the minus twenty means chance would essentially never do this.

143
00:13:51,600 --> 00:14:00,100
Second — and this is the one people forget — a bigger database inflates E for exactly the same alignment, because n is in the formula.

144
00:14:00,100 --> 00:14:03,125
Nothing about the alignment has changed.

145
00:14:03,125 --> 00:14:06,575
The opportunities for chance to produce it have.

146
00:14:06,575 --> 00:14:19,100
So an E-value is only meaningful together with the database it was computed against, and the same alignment can be convincingly significant against a small database and unremarkable against all of GenBank.

147
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:21,725
This is also the textbook form.

148
00:14:21,725 --> 00:14:30,450
Real BLAST uses slightly smaller effective lengths, since an alignment cannot start right at the end of a sequence, and corrects for composition.

149
00:14:30,450 --> 00:14:32,800
The behaviour is the same.

150
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:38,575
Both numbers appear in every BLAST output, and they answer different questions.

151
00:14:38,575 --> 00:14:44,175
A raw score depends on the scoring system, so it cannot be compared between searches.

152
00:14:44,175 --> 00:14:51,675
The bit score fixes that: S-prime equals lambda S minus the natural log of K, all over the natural log of two.

153
00:14:51,675 --> 00:15:02,550
That normalises the scoring system away, so bit scores from completely different searches — even searches using different substitution matrices — can be compared directly.

154
00:15:02,550 --> 00:15:06,600
What the bit score does not do is tell you about significance.

155
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:17,725
There is no universal bit-score cutoff, because whether a score is surprising depends on how large a space it was found in, and the bit score deliberately knows nothing about the database.

156
00:15:17,725 --> 00:15:23,200
So: the bit score measures the quality of the alignment, independent of where you found it.

157
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:27,250
The E-value measures how surprised you should be to find it there.

158
00:15:27,250 --> 00:15:33,850
Use the bit score to compare alignments across searches; read the E-value to decide whether to believe one.

159
00:15:33,850 --> 00:15:40,750
And since the E-value moves with database size while the bit score does not, keeping both is genuinely useful.

160
00:15:41,350 --> 00:15:48,575
Here is a real BLASTP search: human beta-globin against its five closest human globin relatives.

161
00:15:48,575 --> 00:15:59,525
Delta-globin comes back at ninety-three point two per cent identity, with an E-value of nine times ten to the minus one hundred and five and a bit score of two hundred and eighty-four.

162
00:15:59,525 --> 00:16:05,100
Epsilon-globin: seventy-five and a half per cent, ten to the minus eighty-seven.

163
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:10,050
Alpha-globin: forty-three point four per cent, ten to the minus thirty-eight.

164
00:16:10,050 --> 00:16:15,200
Zeta-globin: thirty-five point nine per cent, ten to the minus thirty-two.

165
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:17,350
Four confident hits.

166
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:28,700
Note how the identity falls while the E-values stay astronomically small — even forty-three per cent identity over a full globin is far beyond anything chance produces.

167
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:30,725
Then myoglobin.

168
00:16:30,725 --> 00:16:36,475
Sixteen per cent identity, bit score ten point eight, and an E-value of sixty-three.

169
00:16:36,475 --> 00:16:38,150
Sixty-three.

170
00:16:38,150 --> 00:16:43,575
Taken at face value, that is not weak evidence — it is no evidence whatsoever.

171
00:16:43,575 --> 00:16:47,125
You would expect sixty-three hits this good by chance.

172
00:16:47,125 --> 00:16:50,325
But myoglobin genuinely is a globin.

173
00:16:50,325 --> 00:17:01,175
It has the globin fold, it binds haem, and it shares a real evolutionary origin with haemoglobin — we built the tree in Session four and saw the duplication that separates them.

174
00:17:01,175 --> 00:17:03,875
The relationship is not in doubt.

175
00:17:03,875 --> 00:17:11,550
So before the next slide, sit with the question: why does BLAST report essentially nothing for a relationship we know is real?

176
00:17:12,150 --> 00:17:16,000
Here is the answer, and it is not about statistics.

177
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,600
It is about what BLAST looked at.

178
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:23,675
That E-value of sixty-three correctly evaluates the alignment BLAST found.

179
00:17:23,675 --> 00:17:28,475
The problem is the alignment BLAST found is not the best one available.

180
00:17:28,475 --> 00:17:46,925
Run the same search with the two-hit rule switched off — window size zero in BLAST-plus, so that every single seed gets extended — and BLAST finds a full-length alignment: a hundred and forty-five residues at twenty-five and a half per cent identity, with an E-value of five times ten to the minus eleven.

181
00:17:46,925 --> 00:17:48,750
Not marginal.

182
00:17:48,750 --> 00:17:50,400
Convincing.

183
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:52,975
The alignment was always there.

184
00:17:52,975 --> 00:18:03,500
With the default settings, no two seeds happened to fall close enough together on the right diagonal, so that region never got extended, and the good alignment was never found.

185
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:08,200
The statistics were never wrong; they were computed on the wrong alignment.

186
00:18:08,200 --> 00:18:12,125
This is the price of the bargain we made at the start of the block.

187
00:18:12,125 --> 00:18:17,600
BLAST is fast because it skips most of the search, and occasionally it skips the answer.

188
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:22,325
Smith-Waterman, with its guarantee of optimality, would not have missed this.

189
00:18:22,325 --> 00:18:25,750
The practical lesson is not "distrust BLAST".

190
00:18:25,750 --> 00:18:35,300
It is that a poor E-value has two possible meanings — there is nothing there, or BLAST did not look in the right place — and they are indistinguishable from the output.

191
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:45,375
If you have an independent reason to expect a relationship and BLAST reports nothing, that is a reason to search harder, not to conclude the relationship is absent.

192
00:18:45,975 --> 00:18:52,750
One more thing about that myoglobin alignment, which brings the database-size point back with real numbers.

193
00:18:52,750 --> 00:18:59,775
The E-value of five times ten to the minus eleven was computed against a database of five sequences.

194
00:18:59,775 --> 00:19:06,100
That is a ludicrously small database — essentially no opportunity for chance to produce anything.

195
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:18,125
Take the identical alignment, the same hundred and forty-five residues at the same identity, and evaluate it against UniProtKB instead, about nine times ten to the tenth residues.

196
00:19:18,125 --> 00:19:22,475
The E-value becomes roughly zero point zero zero seven.

197
00:19:22,475 --> 00:19:24,600
Still significant.

198
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:33,350
But only just — and from ten to the minus eleven to seven in a thousand is eight orders of magnitude, purely from the size of the haystack.

199
00:19:33,350 --> 00:19:35,850
The alignment never changed.

200
00:19:35,850 --> 00:19:38,175
And then the sobering part.

201
00:19:38,175 --> 00:19:48,000
Relatives that have diverged further than myoglobin — the bacterial truncated haemoglobins, for instance — give no detectable pairwise signal at all.

202
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:56,100
Not a weak signal that better parameters would rescue: no signal, even with the optimal alignment and no heuristic in the way.

203
00:19:56,100 --> 00:19:59,350
Those proteins still share the globin fold.

204
00:19:59,350 --> 00:20:02,275
Session six shows you their structures.

205
00:20:02,275 --> 00:20:11,025
The relationship is real and pairwise sequence comparison simply cannot see it, because too much sequence has changed while the fold stayed put.

206
00:20:11,025 --> 00:20:16,725
That is the wall this block ends at, and it is what the second block exists to get past.

207
00:20:16,725 --> 00:20:20,200
If one sequence is not enough, use the family.

208
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:27,275
Before the profiles, a word about scale, because BLAST's own bargain has been renegotiated since.

209
00:20:27,275 --> 00:20:33,725
BLAST was designed in nineteen ninety, when protein databases held tens of thousands of sequences.

210
00:20:33,725 --> 00:20:37,175
Metagenomic collections now hold billions.

211
00:20:37,175 --> 00:20:43,175
At that scale even BLAST is too slow, and the reason is structural rather than incidental.

212
00:20:43,175 --> 00:20:45,825
Look again at how BLAST works.

213
00:20:45,825 --> 00:20:52,400
It builds a lookup table from the query and its neighbour words, and then reads through every database sequence.

214
00:20:52,400 --> 00:21:03,425
The database scan is linear in the database size, which was fine when the database was small — but it happens again for every single query, and nothing is reused between queries.

215
00:21:03,425 --> 00:21:10,525
MMseqs2, from Steinegger and Söding in twenty seventeen, keeps the logic and turns that part around.

216
00:21:10,525 --> 00:21:21,600
It indexes the target database by short words — k-mers, typically six or seven residues for proteins — mapping each k-mer to the sequences and positions where it occurs.

217
00:21:21,600 --> 00:21:26,075
That index is built once and reused for every query thereafter.

218
00:21:26,075 --> 00:21:37,075
Then, for each k-mer in the query, it generates the similar k-mers, scored with a substitution matrix exactly as BLAST generates neighbour words, and looks them up in the index.

219
00:21:37,075 --> 00:21:42,000
The query no longer reads the database; it asks the index where to look.

220
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,975
Same seed-extend-evaluate idea.

221
00:21:44,975 --> 00:21:47,775
Same substitution matrices.

222
00:21:47,775 --> 00:21:50,525
No machine learning anywhere in it.

223
00:21:50,525 --> 00:21:59,350
The difference is purely which side gets indexed, and it is worth noticing how much follows from a decision that sounds like an implementation detail.

224
00:21:59,950 --> 00:22:08,100
What MMseqs2 does after the lookup is the other half of the speed, and it is the funnel idea from BLAST taken much further.

225
00:22:08,100 --> 00:22:17,375
First filter: keep only database sequences where two matching k-mers fall on the same diagonal — the same offset between query and target position.

226
00:22:17,375 --> 00:22:27,775
That is exactly the dot-plot idea from Session four: two consistent hits lying on one diagonal are far stronger evidence of a real alignment than one hit anywhere.

227
00:22:27,775 --> 00:22:30,225
Most candidates die here.

228
00:22:30,225 --> 00:22:37,300
Second filter: score the survivors with a quick ungapped alignment along that diagonal, and discard the weak ones.

229
00:22:37,300 --> 00:22:49,150
Only then, on the small set remaining, run the expensive gapped Smith-Waterman alignment — heavily optimised to use the processor's vector instructions, so that even the expensive step is fast.

230
00:22:49,150 --> 00:22:54,450
A cascade of ever more expensive filters, each passing on a small fraction.

231
00:22:54,450 --> 00:22:59,950
The result is often hundreds of times faster than BLAST at similar sensitivity.

232
00:22:59,950 --> 00:23:02,875
Two things follow for the rest of the course.

233
00:23:02,875 --> 00:23:09,975
Like PSI-BLAST, which is next, MMseqs2 can iterate — turn its hits into a profile and search again.

234
00:23:09,975 --> 00:23:16,750
And it is what ColabFold uses to build the alignments AlphaFold needs, which you meet in Session twelve.

235
00:23:16,750 --> 00:23:22,675
The speed of this search is part of why predicting a structure is now a thing you do in an afternoon.

236
00:23:22,675 --> 00:23:27,625
Session six meets its structural counterpart, Foldseek, from the same group.

